<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1386">1386</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Raad van Vlaanderen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> was the high court and governing council of the County of Flanders, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, in 1386. Seated first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, it heard appeals and cases touching the count's rights and supervised local justice. It survived under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> as the county's supreme court until the French annexation of 1795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Council of Flanders" width="290" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raad_van_vlaanderen.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The council grew out of the less formal justice that the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and their advisers had long exercised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> sought to turn this into a permanent court with written rules and a fixed jurisdiction at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, at the same time as he set up the Lille Chamber of Accounts to audit government spending. The cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franc-of-bruges">Franc of Bruges</a>, however, refused to accept that the council could hear appeals against their own magistrates.</p><p>The court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> in 1407, where it sat mainly in the Gravensteen castle, and on 17 August 1409 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a> gave it statutes in 44 articles. Article 26 required French in closed hearings and left the choice of language to the parties in open ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> was at odds with the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in the 15th century, the council often sat elsewhere, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a>, Dendermonde, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, until it was settled again in Ghent in 1498. The counts had accepted appeals to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> from the parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> anciently attached to the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, essentially those west of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, but in 1521 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-mechelen">Great Council of Mechelen</a> sole appellate jurisdiction over the whole county, and by the Treaty of Cambrai of 1529 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> gave up all claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> or jurisdiction there.</p><p>During the Dutch Revolt Calvinists seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> in 1578, and from 1580 to 1585 the council sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a> under the presidency of Willem van Pamele, loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, while a rival president, Adolf van Meetkercke, served the side in revolt. In 1694 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> disbanded the Admiralty Council and gave its maritime jurisdiction to the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. The council pronounced its last sentence of death on 24 July 1772. Among its first presidents were Pieter van der Zijpe, from 1386, and Simon van Formelis, who held the office from 1409 to 1440.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Flanders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Burgundian dukes added a <b>Council of Flanders</b>, founded by Philip the Bold in 1386, as the county’s high court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
